 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.civilizaciones.02.mexicas-se-impersonal",
-  "title": "Se impersonal en descripciones históricas",
+  "title": "Preterite and imperfect: events and background",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Impersonal *se* lets a historical text state a fact or a common practice without naming who does it — useful when the subject is everyone, unknown, or simply beside the point. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the *indefinido* and the *imperfecto* from A2 Unit 22. The preterite tells what happened (*fundaron Tenochtitlan*); the imperfect tells what was going on or what used to happen (*la ciudad crecía*, *cultivaban en chinampas*). *Mientras* often links an imperfect to a preterite, as you know from *cuando* and *mientras* in A2 Unit 13."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Hoy se suele hablar de 'imperio mexica' aunque, en sentido estricto, gobernaban tres ciudades juntas.",
-          "english": "Today people usually talk about a 'Mexica empire,' though strictly speaking three cities governed together."
+          "spanish": "Hacia 1325 los mexicas fundaron Tenochtitlan.",
+          "english": "Around 1325 the Mexica founded Tenochtitlan."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se sabe con gran detalle qué se exigía a cada región gracias al Códice Mendoza.",
-          "english": "It's known in great detail what was demanded of each region, thanks to the Codex Mendoza."
+          "spanish": "Mientras la ciudad crecía, los mexicas construyeron calzadas y canales.",
+          "english": "While the city was growing, the Mexica built causeways and canals."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En las chinampas se cultivaban varias cosechas al año.",
-          "english": "On the chinampas, several harvests a year were grown."
+          "spanish": "Cultivaban en chinampas y obtenían maíz y frijol.",
+          "english": "They farmed in chinampas and got maize and beans."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se calcula que casi dos millones de personas hablan náhuatl en México en la actualidad.",
-          "english": "It's estimated that almost two million people speak Nahuatl in Mexico today."
+          "spanish": "En 1428 formaron una alianza con Texcoco y Tlacopan.",
+          "english": "In 1428 they formed an alliance with Texcoco and Tlacopan."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Impersonal *se* + third-person singular verb (*se sabe, se calcula, se suele hablar*) works with a verb that has no clear direct object. When there IS a direct object being acted on (*se cultivaban varias cosechas*), the verb agrees with that object instead — this is technically the passive *se*, but in practice historical writing blurs the two constantly."
+      "content": "Ask yourself: is this a single event that moves the story on (preterite), or the scene around it (imperfect)? The imperfect is also the tense for habits in the past, so *cultivaban*, *pagaban* and *tenían* describe how things normally worked."
     }
   ]
 }
