 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.llegadaeuropeos.02.encuentro",
-  "title": "Conectores para ordenar y contrastar acontecimientos",
+  "title": "Sin embargo and no obstante: contrasting two ideas",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Al principio* / *sin embargo* sets up a contrast between an early state and what came after; *mientras* and *al mismo tiempo* show two things happening in parallel; *después* moves the story forward in time. Together they let a text track several developments without a strict single timeline. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know contrast connectors like *sin embargo* and *no obstante* from B1 Unit 1. They begin the second sentence and say that it goes against what you might expect after the first: *Al principio el encuentro fue pacífico. Sin embargo, pronto empezaron los conflictos.*"
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Al principio, el encuentro fue relativamente pacífico. Sin embargo, esa primera impresión cambió con rapidez.",
-          "english": "At first, the encounter was relatively peaceful. However, that first impression changed quickly."
+          "spanish": "Al principio el encuentro fue pacífico. Sin embargo, pronto empezaron los conflictos.",
+          "english": "At first the encounter was peaceful. However, conflicts soon began."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Mientras los españoles buscaban oro, empezaron a exigir a los taínos que entregaran una cantidad fija de metal.",
-          "english": "While the Spanish searched for gold, they began demanding that the Taíno hand over a fixed amount of metal."
+          "spanish": "Los dos pueblos hablaban lenguas distintas. No obstante, hubo intercambio de palabras.",
+          "english": "The two peoples spoke different languages. Nevertheless, words were exchanged."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Al mismo tiempo, se estableció un sistema conocido como encomienda.",
-          "english": "At the same time, a system known as the encomienda was established."
+          "spanish": "Había poco oro en las islas. Sin embargo, los españoles exigían cada vez más.",
+          "english": "There was little gold on the islands. However, the Spanish demanded more and more."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Después llegó un factor todavía más devastador que la violencia directa: las enfermedades europeas.",
-          "english": "Afterward came a factor even more devastating than direct violence: European diseases."
+          "spanish": "Los taínos recibieron a los españoles con amistad; no obstante, los españoles buscaban oro.",
+          "english": "The Taíno received the Spanish with friendship; nevertheless, the Spanish were looking for gold."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Al principio* always sets up a later contrast — it practically demands a *sin embargo* or *pero* somewhere after it. *Mientras* and *al mismo tiempo* both mark simultaneity, but *mientras* usually opens the sentence with the background action, while *al mismo tiempo* can stand alone at the front of a new sentence."
+      "content": "*Sin embargo* and *no obstante* mean the same; *no obstante* sounds a little more formal. Put a comma after them. In a long sentence they can follow a semicolon, as in the last example. Do not use *por eso* here: that connector shows a result, not a contrast."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-llegadaeuropeos-03-conquista-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-llegadaeuropeos-03-conquista-gr.json
