 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.llegadaeuropeos.03.conquista",
-  "title": "Construcciones con se en la historia",
+  "title": "A pesar de que: concession in history",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Se* shows up in several related but distinct roles in historical writing: passive (*se firmó un acuerdo*), reporting what's said or remembered (*se cuenta que...*), and impersonal fact-stating (*se organizó una resistencia*). All let the text focus on the event, not on naming exactly who did it. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *a pesar de que* and *pese a* from B1 Unit 32: they introduce something that did not stop the main fact. *A pesar de que* is followed by a verb; *a pesar de* is followed by a noun or an infinitive. In history they show resistance against the odds."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Al ver cómo se maltrataba a su pueblo, Hatuey huyó a Cuba con un grupo de seguidores.",
-          "english": "Seeing how his people were being mistreated, Hatuey fled to Cuba with a group of followers."
+          "spanish": "A pesar de que eran menos, Hatuey y sus hombres resistieron.",
+          "english": "Although they were fewer, Hatuey and his men resisted."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Allí se organizó una resistencia armada contra los españoles.",
-          "english": "There, an armed resistance against the Spanish was organized."
+          "spanish": "A pesar de la resistencia, la población taína bajó mucho.",
+          "english": "Despite the resistance, the Taíno population fell sharply."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se cuenta que Hatuey preguntó si los españoles también iban a ese cielo.",
-          "english": "It's said that Hatuey asked whether the Spanish also went to that heaven."
+          "spanish": "A pesar de que los españoles enviaron varias expediciones, no pudieron derrotar a Enriquillo.",
+          "english": "Although the Spanish sent several expeditions, they could not defeat Enriquillo."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "En 1533 se firmó un acuerdo de paz que reconocía la libertad de Enriquillo y de su gente.",
-          "english": "In 1533 a peace agreement was signed recognizing the freedom of Enriquillo and his people."
+          "spanish": "Pese a las leyes, los abusos continuaron.",
+          "english": "Despite the laws, the abuses went on."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Se cuenta que* / *se dice que* is the historian's hedge — useful exactly when a detail comes from oral tradition or a later chronicle rather than a document written at the time, as with Hatuey's famous last words. It signals \"this is what's reported,\" not \"this is certainly what happened.\""
+      "content": "After *a pesar de que* use a verb in the indicative for facts that happened (*eran*, *enviaron*). After *a pesar de* use a noun (*la resistencia*) or an infinitive (*a pesar de ser menos*). *Pese a* is a shorter, more formal way to say the same."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-llegadaeuropeos-04-nuevos-mundos-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-llegadaeuropeos-04-nuevos-mundos-gr.json
