     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "La prosa histórica y académica conecta ideas con expresiones más formales que las que se usan en la conversación diaria. Estas frases están tomadas directamente de la lectura anterior."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the pluperfect with *había* from B1 Unit 1: *había* + past participle says that something had already happened before another moment in the past. In history this shows what came first: *Cuando llegaron los europeos, los pueblos americanos ya habían domesticado el maíz.*"
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "A lo largo de casi quince mil kilómetros, cientos de pueblos distintos habían desarrollado formas de vida adaptadas a paisajes completamente diferentes.",
-          "english": "Along nearly fifteen thousand kilometres, hundreds of distinct peoples had developed ways of life adapted to completely different landscapes."
+          "spanish": "Cuando los primeros grupos llegaron a Sudamérica, ya habían recorrido miles de kilómetros.",
+          "english": "When the first groups reached South America, they had already travelled thousands of kilometres."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Las enfermedades mataron a tanta gente, y tan rápido, que ni siquiera hubo tiempo de contarla.",
-          "english": "The diseases killed so many people, and so fast, that there wasn't even time to count them."
+          "spanish": "Mucho antes de los mayas, ya habían existido otras civilizaciones.",
+          "english": "Long before the Maya, other civilisations had already existed."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El maíz terminó cultivándose a lo largo de todo el continente, mientras que la papa se convirtió en la base de la alimentación andina.",
-          "english": "Maize ended up being grown all along the continent, whereas the potato became the basis of the Andean diet."
+          "spanish": "En 1492 los pueblos americanos ya habían domesticado el maíz y la papa.",
+          "english": "By 1492 the peoples of the Americas had already domesticated maize and the potato."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Mucho antes de que existieran los mayas, los mexica o los incas, ya se habían desarrollado civilizaciones influyentes.",
-          "english": "Long before the Maya, Mexica or Inca existed, influential civilisations had already developed."
+          "spanish": "Los cazadores todavía no habían formado aldeas.",
+          "english": "The hunters had not yet formed villages."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Mientras que* contrasts two things (\"whereas\"), not just two things happening at once — that's plain *mientras*. *A lo largo de* works for distance and for time (\"along fifteen thousand kilometres\" or \"along three centuries\"), which is why it recurs so often in historical writing."
+      "content": "The pluperfect is built like the present perfect from A2 Unit 1, but with *había*, *habías*, *había*, *habíamos*, *habían*. The participle never changes (*habían domesticado*). *Ya* and *todavía no* from A2 Unit 2 fit well: they show that one thing came before another."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-02-civilization-se-impersonal-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-02-civilization-se-impersonal-gr.json
index 80a0fedb..6b2f53b0 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-02-civilization-se-impersonal-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-02-civilization-se-impersonal-gr.json
@@ -1,39 +1,37 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.precolombina.02.civilization-se-impersonal",
-  "title": "Se pasiva / impersonal, y voz pasiva",
+  "title": "The passive with se: what was built and used",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Los textos históricos evitan nombrar a un sujeto concreto cuando no se sabe quién hizo algo, o cuando lo importante es el hecho mismo y no quién lo causó. El español tiene dos herramientas para esto: el se pasivo/impersonal (*se organizaron*, *se convirtió*, *se debate*) y la voz pasiva con ser + participio (*fueron abandonadas*). Las frases siguientes están tomadas directamente de la lectura anterior."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the passive with *se* from B1 Unit 6: *se* + a third-person verb says what was done without saying who did it. The verb agrees with the thing: *se construyó una ciudad*, *se construyeron ciudades*. It is useful for outlining a whole civilisation."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Los mayas se organizaron en decenas de ciudades-estado independientes.",
-          "english": "The Maya organised themselves into dozens of independent city-states."
+          "spanish": "En Mesoamérica se construyeron pirámides de piedra.",
+          "english": "In Mesoamerica, stone pyramids were built."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchas ciudades mayas del sur fueron abandonadas repentinamente.",
-          "english": "Many Maya cities in the south were suddenly abandoned."
+          "spanish": "En los Andes se usaban quipus para llevar las cuentas.",
+          "english": "In the Andes, quipus were used to keep accounts."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Todavía se debate por qué fueron abandonadas.",
-          "english": "It is still debated why they were abandoned."
+          "spanish": "Tenochtitlan se fundó hacia 1325.",
+          "english": "Tenochtitlan was founded around 1325."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cuzco se convirtió en el centro político y religioso de todo el imperio.",
-          "english": "Cuzco became the political and religious centre of the whole empire."
-        },
-        {
-          "spanish": "La información de los quipus todavía no se ha logrado descifrar del todo.",
-          "english": "The information in the quipus still hasn't been fully deciphered."
+          "spanish": "En las dos regiones se cultivaba el maíz.",
+          "english": "In both regions, maize was grown."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Se* + verb keeps the action but drops the person entirely — useful when nobody knows, or nobody needs to say, who did it. *Ser* + participio keeps a passive meaning too, but reads slightly more formal and pairs naturally with *por* if a cause does get named later (*fueron abandonadas por la sequía*, if a historian wanted to commit to that explanation). Neither is more \"correct\" than the other; which one a text reaches for is mostly a matter of register and rhythm."
+      "content": "Use the preterite for a single event (*se fundó*) and the imperfect for something that went on or was habitual (*se usaban*, *se cultivaba*). The verb is plural when the thing is plural: *se construyeron pirámides*, never *se construyó pirámides*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-03-power-passive-voice-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-03-power-passive-voice-gr.json
index a8a9e1f5..da940072 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-03-power-passive-voice-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-03-power-passive-voice-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,37 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.precolombina.03.power-passive-voice",
-  "title": "Se pasiva para describir instituciones",
+  "title": "Relative clauses: adding information about people",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Cuando un texto describe cómo funcionaba una institución —cómo se gobernaba, cómo se organizaba el trabajo, cómo se trataba a los territorios conquistados—, lo habitual es usar el se pasivo en vez de nombrar a un sujeto concreto: no importa quién exactamente lo hacía, sino cómo funcionaba el sistema. Estas frases están tomadas directamente de la lectura anterior."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know relative clauses with *que* and *quien* from B1 Unit 5. *Que* joins a second piece of information to a noun. With commas it adds extra information about someone you already know (*el Inca, que gobernaba desde Cuzco*); without commas it says which people you mean (*los guerreros que capturaban enemigos*)."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "En el imperio inca, el poder se organizaba de forma muy distinta.",
-          "english": "In the Inca empire, power was organised very differently."
+          "spanish": "El Inca, que gobernaba desde Cuzco, era el jefe del Estado.",
+          "english": "The Inca, who ruled from Cuzco, was the head of the state."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se exigía tributo a las ciudades sometidas, pero rara vez se sustituía a sus gobernantes locales.",
-          "english": "Tribute was demanded from the subjugated cities, but their local rulers were rarely replaced."
+          "spanish": "Los guerreros que capturaban enemigos recibían honores.",
+          "english": "The warriors who captured enemies received honours."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "La sangre derramada se ofrecía a los dioses.",
-          "english": "The spilled blood was offered to the gods."
+          "spanish": "Los mexicas tenían un gobernante, el tlatoani, que dirigía la ciudad.",
+          "english": "The Mexica had a ruler, the tlatoani, who led the city."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El ritual se representaba en estelas de piedra que todavía pueden verse hoy.",
-          "english": "The ritual was depicted on stone stelae that can still be seen today."
+          "spanish": "Los campesinos, que eran la mayoría, pagaban tributo con trabajo.",
+          "english": "The peasants, who were the majority, paid tribute with work."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Notice the verb agrees with what follows: *se exigía tributo* (singular) but *se representaban rituales* would need the plural — the thing being organised, offered or demanded is grammatically the subject, even though no person is ever named."
+      "content": "With people, *que* is the normal choice and does not change for gender or number. With commas the sentence is still true if you remove the clause; without commas it is not: not every warrior received honours, only those who captured enemies."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-04-daily-life-gerund-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-04-daily-life-gerund-gr.json
index 605b4852..207eacec 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-04-daily-life-gerund-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-04-daily-life-gerund-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,37 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.precolombina.04.daily-life-gerund",
-  "title": "Gerundio para acción paralela",
+  "title": "Soler: what people used to do",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "El gerundio (-ando, -iendo) describe una acción que ocurre al mismo tiempo que otra, o el método por el que algo se logra —una forma compacta de unir dos ideas sin usar un conector. Estas frases están tomadas directamente de la lectura anterior."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *soler* from B1 Unit 5. *Soler* + infinitive says that something happens or happened regularly. To talk about everyday life in the past you use the imperfect: *solía comer*, *solían cultivar*. It is a good way to describe habits in a world that no longer exists."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Cocinando los granos con cal o ceniza, los pueblos mesoamericanos liberaban nutrientes que el maíz crudo no ofrece.",
-          "english": "By cooking the grains with lime or ash, Mesoamerican peoples released nutrients raw maize doesn't offer."
+          "spanish": "Los campesinos solían cultivar maíz, frijol y calabaza.",
+          "english": "The farmers used to grow maize, beans and squash."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El chocolate se preparaba batiendo cacao molido con agua, chile y especias hasta formar espuma.",
-          "english": "Chocolate was prepared by beating ground cacao with water, chilli and spices until it foamed."
+          "spanish": "En los Andes la gente solía comer papa y quinua.",
+          "english": "In the Andes people used to eat potatoes and quinoa."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Masticando hojas de coca, los habitantes de las alturas combatían el hambre, el frío y el mal de altura.",
-          "english": "By chewing coca leaves, people in the highlands fought off hunger, cold and altitude sickness."
+          "spanish": "La gente solía tomar una bebida amarga de cacao.",
+          "english": "People used to drink a bitter cacao drink."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Comerciando, tejiendo o cultivando, la mayoría de la gente también encontraba tiempo para el juego de pelota.",
-          "english": "Trading, weaving or farming, most people also found time for the ballgame."
+          "spanish": "Los jugadores solían usar una pelota de caucho.",
+          "english": "The players used to use a rubber ball."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "A gerund at the start of a sentence (*Cocinando los granos...*, *Masticando hojas...*) usually answers \"how?\" or \"by doing what?\" — it's doing the job *al + infinitivo* or *porque* would do, just more compactly. It never changes form for gender or number: *comiendo, vistiéndose y jugando* stays exactly like that regardless of who's doing it."
+      "content": "*Solía* goes with *yo*, *él*, *ella*; *solían* with *ellos*, *ellas*, *ustedes*. The verb after it is always an infinitive. Do not use *soler* in the preterite for a single event: it describes habits, not one-time actions."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-05-synthesis-connectors-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-05-synthesis-connectors-gr.json
index 059bf84e..1dad18c1 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-05-synthesis-connectors-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-precolombina-05-synthesis-connectors-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,37 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.precolombina.05.synthesis-connectors",
-  "title": "Conectores para comparar evidencia",
+  "title": "Reporting what experts say",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*A pesar de* introduces a contrast the writer chooses not to let win — a fact stands despite an obstacle. *A raíz de* introduces a cause: something happened as a direct result of an earlier event. Historians reach for both constantly, because weighing evidence almost always means admitting a contradiction (*a pesar de*) or tracing a consequence (*a raíz de*). These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know how to report information and to cite a source with *según* from B1 Unit 13. In history almost every fact comes from a source, so you often name it: *según los arqueólogos…*, *los estudios indican que…*. The verb after *que* is in the indicative."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "A pesar de que los mayas escribían con un sistema jeroglífico completo, hoy solo sobreviven cuatro libros mayas originales.",
-          "english": "Despite the fact that the Maya wrote with a complete hieroglyphic system, today only four original Maya books survive."
+          "spanish": "Según los especialistas, solo se conservan cuatro códices mayas.",
+          "english": "According to the specialists, only four Maya codices survive."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A raíz de ese acto, y de otras destrucciones similares durante la conquista, casi toda la literatura maya escrita desapareció para siempre.",
-          "english": "As a result of that act, and other similar destructions during the conquest, almost all written Maya literature disappeared forever."
+          "spanish": "Los arqueólogos dicen que excavan ciudades y tumbas.",
+          "english": "The archaeologists say they excavate cities and tombs."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A pesar de las lagunas y las pérdidas, cada fuente aporta una pieza distinta.",
-          "english": "Despite the gaps and the losses, each source contributes a distinct piece."
+          "spanish": "Los estudios indican que había miles de estructuras bajo el bosque.",
+          "english": "Studies indicate that there were thousands of structures under the forest."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A raíz de descubrimientos recientes, la imagen del mundo precolombino sigue cambiando.",
-          "english": "As a result of recent discoveries, the picture of the pre-Columbian world keeps changing."
+          "spanish": "Según un estudio publicado en 2018, la tecnología LiDAR encontró muchas estructuras.",
+          "english": "According to a study published in 2018, LiDAR technology found many structures."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Both connectors take a noun or a full clause with *que*: *a pesar de + [noun / que + clause]*, *a raíz de + [noun]*. Don't confuse *a pesar de* (despite, a contrast) with *a causa de* (because of, a plain cause) — *a raíz de* sits between the two: a cause with a sense of \"and everything changed after.\""
+      "content": "*Según* names the source and takes no *que*: *según los especialistas*. With a verb you need *que*: *los especialistas indican que*. Naming the source shows that a claim is not yours, which matters in history, where experts often disagree."
     }
   ]
 }
